      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=-t^{3}+4$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=-t^{3}+4$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(-t^{3}+4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(-t^{3}+4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -\\int t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+4\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -\\frac{t^{3+1}}{(3+1)}+4\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+C \\\\ &= -\\frac{t^{4}}{4}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
